@page
@model PrivacyModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Setup";
}

<section class="setup-page">
    <h1>Azure Portal setup</h1>
    <p>This page lists every Azure Portal value needed by the app. It uses placeholders only; do not paste real secrets into source code.</p>

    <div class="panel">
        <h2>1. App registration</h2>
        <p>Create or open the app registration used by this web app.</p>
        <pre><code>Azure Portal
App registrations
New registration</code></pre>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Name:</strong> any friendly name, for example <code>MicrosoftPilot</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Supported account types:</strong> choose <code>Accounts in any organizational directory and personal Microsoft accounts</code>, or <code>Personal Microsoft accounts only</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Platform:</strong> <code>Web</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
        <h2>2. Required app identifiers</h2>
        <p>Find these under <code>App registrations -> your app -> Overview</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>AzureAd:TenantId = consumers
AzureAd:ClientId = YOUR_APPLICATION_CLIENT_ID
AzureAd:CallbackPath = /signin-oidc</code></pre>
        <p>Use <code>consumers</code> for personal Microsoft accounts. Use <code>common</code> only if you intentionally support both work/school and personal accounts.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
        <h2>3. Redirect URIs</h2>
        <p>Find these under <code>Authentication -> Platform configurations -> Web</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>Local:
https://localhost:5001/signin-oidc

Azure App Service:
https://YOUR_APP_SERVICE_NAME.azurewebsites.net/signin-oidc</code></pre>
        <p>The URI must match exactly, including <code>https</code>, port, host name, and <code>/signin-oidc</code>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
        <h2>4. Client secret</h2>
        <p>Create this under <code>Certificates &amp; secrets -> Client secrets -> New client secret</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>AzureAd:ClientSecret = YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET_VALUE</code></pre>
        <p>Copy the secret <strong>Value</strong>, not the Secret ID. The Value is shown only once.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
        <h2>5. Microsoft Graph API permissions</h2>
        <p>Find these under <code>API permissions -> Add a permission -> Microsoft Graph -> Delegated permissions</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>User.Read
Mail.Read
Calendars.Read
Files.Read</code></pre>
        <p>No Teams permissions are required. The app reads only Outlook mail, calendar meetings, account profile, and OneDrive Word document metadata.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
        <h2>6. App Service environment variables</h2>
        <p>For Azure hosting, set these under <code>App Service -> Settings -> Environment variables</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>AzureAd__TenantId = consumers
AzureAd__ClientId = YOUR_APPLICATION_CLIENT_ID
AzureAd__ClientSecret = YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET_VALUE
ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT = Production</code></pre>
        <p>Use double underscores in App Service settings. Do not use colons there.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
        <h2>7. App Service authentication</h2>
        <p>Find this under <code>App Service -> Authentication</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>App Service authentication = Off</code></pre>
        <p>The ASP.NET Core code handles Microsoft sign-in with <code>Microsoft.Identity.Web</code>. Turning on App Service Authentication would create a second login system.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
        <h2>8. Local development secrets</h2>
        <p>For local Visual Studio debugging, store real values in user-secrets instead of <code>appsettings.json</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>cd E:\microsoftpilot
dotnet user-secrets init
dotnet user-secrets set "AzureAd:TenantId" "consumers"
dotnet user-secrets set "AzureAd:ClientId" "YOUR_APPLICATION_CLIENT_ID"
dotnet user-secrets set "AzureAd:ClientSecret" "YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET_VALUE"</code></pre>
    </div>
</section>
